      <div class="qa"><div class="q">What is the Best Answer Hub guide to free AI voice overs for commercial use?</div><div class="a">This Best Answer Hub playbook is a license-first guide to AI narration you can legally sell. It reads the terms at source rather than repeating listicles, sorts the free options into safe, conditional and non-commercial, and covers what YouTube actually requires for a synthetic voice.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Which free text to speech can be used commercially?</div><div class="a">Kokoro-82M under Apache 2.0, Chatterbox and MeloTTS under MIT, and Google Cloud's free monthly allowance under its commercial terms. Each grants commercial use and none requires attribution on the audio. The famous free tiers are usually the ones that do not, which is the reverse of what most people assume.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Can you use the ElevenLabs free plan for commercial use?</div><div class="a">No. ElevenLabs states that the free plan does not include a commercial license and cannot be used for any commercial purpose, and its terms of use restrict free users to non-commercial purposes. Even non-commercial sharing requires crediting elevenlabs.io in the title. Starter at $6 a month is the cheapest tier that grants commercial rights.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Is Kokoro TTS free for commercial use?</div><div class="a">Yes. Kokoro-82M is published under Apache 2.0, which permits commercial use, and its model card states it has been deployed in commercial products. One 82-million-parameter model carries 54 voices across 8 languages. Five voices, four Japanese and one French, trace to CC BY sources; every English voice is clear.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">What is a genuinely free alternative to ElevenLabs?</div><div class="a">A local open-weight model, or Google Cloud's free tier. Kokoro-82M runs on an ordinary processor with no account and no per-character cost. Google Cloud gives four million Standard characters a month free with full commercial rights. Both avoid the free-tier commercial restriction that applies at ElevenLabs.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Is there royalty free AI voice for YouTube?</div><div class="a">Yes, if you choose on license rather than on price. Audio generated from an Apache 2.0 or MIT model carries no royalty, no attribution and no per-use fee, which is what people usually mean by royalty free. Check the model license and, for Piper, the individual voice license, because those differ.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Does an AI voice stop a YouTube channel being monetized?</div><div class="a">No. YouTube's policy targets content that is templated and interchangeable, not narration that happens to be synthetic. What is disallowed is AI-generated content made with generic or unoriginal templates giving the impression of mass production. Original research and writing with a synthetic voice remains eligible.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Do you have to disclose an AI voice over on YouTube?</div><div class="a">Usually not. YouTube requires disclosure when AI meaningfully alters or generates photorealistic content, and its list of exemptions names cloning your own voice for voice overs or dubs. A synthetic stock narration over your own footage is not on the disclosure list. Meta's rule is broader and does cover realistic-sounding generated audio.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Is Coqui XTTS-v2 free for commercial use?</div><div class="a">No, and this is the most common mistake in AI voice licensing. The Coqui Public Model License permits only non-commercial use of the model and its outputs, and defines non-commercial as use from which you receive no direct or indirect payment. The restriction follows the audio file, not just the download.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Is Piper TTS MIT licensed?</div><div class="a">Not any more. The archived repository was MIT; development moved and the current engine is GPL 3.0. More importantly, each Piper voice carries its own license from the dataset that trained it. Several popular English voices are CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, which forbids commercial use, so check the voice rather than the engine.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">How much does an AI voice over cost per million characters?</div><div class="a">From nothing to about $200. A local open-weight model costs only electricity. Google Cloud charges $4 per million characters for Standard voices, $16 for Neural2 and $30 for Chirp 3 HD. ElevenLabs Starter works out near $200 per million, since each character consumes one credit.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">What is the best offline text to speech?</div><div class="a">Kokoro-82M is the usual answer for English narration, because it pairs a permissive Apache 2.0 license with a size that runs on a processor rather than a graphics card. Independent benchmarks on four CPU cores measured roughly two minutes of audio per minute of compute, though results vary widely with thread count.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Can you clone someone else's voice legally?</div><div class="a">Only with documented consent, and sometimes not even then. Microsoft's speech code of conduct forbids impersonation without explicit valid consent and bars simulating politicians even with consent. Tennessee's ELVIS Act protects voice directly, and California, Indiana and Nevada include voice in right-of-publicity law.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Does a free AI voice require attribution?</div><div class="a">It depends on the tool, and attribution is often a condition rather than a courtesy. ElevenLabs requires free-plan output to credit elevenlabs.io in the title. Apache 2.0 and MIT models impose no attribution on generated audio. Kokoro documents five voices, four Japanese and one French, that trace to CC BY sources.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Which AI voice tools cannot be used commercially even though they are free?</div><div class="a">Coqui XTTS-v2, whose license covers its outputs; F5-TTS, whose pre-trained weights are CC BY-NC even though its code is MIT; several Piper voices licensed CC BY-NC-SA; and the ElevenLabs free plan. Murf grants commercial rights but its free trial does not allow downloads at all.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cav">Where these terms came from</div>
    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      <li><a href="https://huggingface.co/hexgrad/Kokoro-82M" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kokoro-82M model card</a> and its <a href="https://huggingface.co/hexgrad/Kokoro-82M/blob/main/VOICES.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">voice documentation</a>: Apache 2.0, 54 voices, 8 languages, and the CC BY credits on four Japanese voices and one French voice.</li>
      <li><a href="https://elevenlabs.io/docs/help-center/legal/can-i-publish-the-content-i-generate-on-the-platform" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ElevenLabs commercial license policy</a> and <a href="https://elevenlabs.io/terms-of-use" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">terms of use</a> (updated 31 March 2026): the free plan grants no commercial license, and requires attribution in the title.</li>
      <li><a href="https://elevenlabs.io/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ElevenLabs pricing</a>: Starter at $6 a month for 30,000 credits, one credit per character.</li>
      <li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/text-to-speech/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Cloud Text-to-Speech pricing</a>: four million free Standard and WaveNet characters a month, then $4, $16 and $30 per million for Standard, Neural2 and Chirp 3 HD.</li>
      <li><a href="https://coqui.ai/cpml" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Coqui Public Model License</a>: non-commercial use of the model "and its outputs".</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/SWivid/F5-TTS" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">F5-TTS</a>: MIT code, CC BY-NC pre-trained models.</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/OHF-Voice/piper1-gpl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Piper (current repository)</a> and its <a href="https://github.com/OHF-Voice/piper1-gpl/blob/main/docs/VOICES.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">voice documentation</a>: GPL 3.0 engine, per-voice licenses, several non-commercial.</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/resemble-ai/chatterbox" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chatterbox</a>: MIT, with a neural watermark in every generated file.</li>
      <li><a href="https://help.murf.ai/is-murf-free-to-use" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Murf free tier</a>: no downloads on the free trial. <a href="https://speechify.com/terms/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Speechify terms</a>: commercial use limited to Voice Over Studio.</li>
      <li><a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/1311392" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube channel monetization policies</a>: the inauthentic content rule and the AI personas restriction.</li>
      <li><a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/14328491" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube altered or synthetic content disclosure</a>: the requirement, and the exemption for cloning your own voice.</li>
      <li><a href="https://transparency.meta.com/policies/community-standards/misinformation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Meta Community Standards on misinformation</a>: disclosure for realistic-sounding generated audio.</li>
      <li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/legal/cognitive-services/speech-service/tts-code-of-conduct" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft code of conduct for text to speech</a> (updated 1 May 2026): consent and impersonation rules.</li>
      <li><a href="https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU AI Act, Article 50</a>: provider marking duty for generated output, and a deployer duty to disclose deep fakes; applicable since 2 August 2026.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="toolsrow">Related reading from Best Answer Hub: <a href="/playbooks/make-videos-with-claude-code/">how to make videos with Claude Code</a>, <a href="/playbooks/ai-tools-worth-paying-for/">which AI tools are worth paying for</a>, and the free <a href="/tools/">Best Answer Hub tools</a>. The pipeline that uses these voices lives at <a href="/ai-agent-skills/">AI Agent Skills</a>.</p>
  </article>
